INSERT INTO CLINICAL_NOTE VALUES
('N001','M001','E001','2025-01-10','progress','Dr. Sarah Chen','SUBJECTIVE: 86 y/o female presents for routine hypertension follow-up. Reports occasional dizziness upon standing. Denies chest pain, SOB, or palpitations. Currently taking Lisinopril 20mg, Amlodipine 10mg, Metformin 1000mg BID, Furosemide 40mg, Warfarin 5mg, and Omeprazole 20mg daily. Reports compliance with all medications. OBJECTIVE: BP 138/82 sitting, 120/70 standing (orthostatic drop noted). HR 72 regular. Weight stable at 142 lbs. ASSESSMENT: 1. Hypertension - borderline controlled with orthostatic component, likely medication-related. 2. Polypharmacy concern - 6 active medications, consider deprescribing review. 3. CKD stage 3 - stable per last labs. PLAN: Continue current regimen. Order comprehensive metabolic panel. Discuss fall risk precautions. Return in 3 months.'),
('N002','M001','E002','2025-03-15','ER','Dr. Alan Wright','CHIEF COMPLAINT: Fall at home. HISTORY: Patient fell while getting up from chair. No LOC. Landed on right hip. Pain 6/10. No head strike per patient. EXAM: Alert, oriented x3. Right hip TTP, no deformity. ROM limited by pain. Neuro intact. IMAGING: X-ray pelvis — no acute fracture. ASSESSMENT: Mechanical fall, likely orthostatic hypotension secondary to polypharmacy (6+ medications including Furosemide and Lisinopril). PLAN: Discharge home with fall precautions. Recommend PCP follow-up for medication review — concern for overtreatment contributing to falls. PT evaluation ordered.'),
('N005','M002','E005','2025-02-28','discharge','Dr. James Wilson','DISCHARGE SUMMARY: 87 y/o male admitted for acute on chronic systolic heart failure exacerbation. BNP 890 on admission. Presented with 3-day history of worsening dyspnea, 8-lb weight gain, and bilateral lower extremity edema. Treated with IV furosemide with good diuresis (net -4L over 3 days). Digoxin level checked and was therapeutic at 1.8 ng/mL. Currently on 8 medications including Warfarin for A-fib — INR therapeutic at 2.8. Diabetes management suboptimal with HbA1c 8.1% — Glipizide increased to 10mg daily. DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS: Metoprolol 50mg BID, Lisinopril 10mg daily, Warfarin 7.5mg daily, Furosemide 80mg BID, Metformin 500mg BID, Glipizide 10mg daily, Atorvastatin 80mg daily, Aspirin 81mg daily, Digoxin 0.125mg daily. Daily weights and 1500mL fluid restriction. Follow-up in 1 week.'),
('N016','M006','E016','2025-01-08','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 91 y/o male with Alzheimer disease, here for quarterly dementia evaluation. Caregiver (daughter) reports increased confusion over past month, occasional agitation in evenings (sundowning). Has had 2 near-falls in the past 2 weeks. Currently taking 9 medications. Patient is mostly non-verbal today. OBJECTIVE: MMSE 12/30 (prev 15/30 six months ago). Gait unsteady, uses walker. ASSESSMENT: 1. Alzheimer disease — progressive decline. MMSE dropped 3 points in 6 months. 2. CRITICAL POLYPHARMACY — 9 active medications in 91-year-old with dementia. Warfarin + Aspirin combination raises significant bleeding risk. Sertraline may worsen confusion/falls. 3. Fall risk — HIGH. PLAN: Urgent deprescribing review. Consider stopping Aspirin (dual antithrombotic unnecessary with Warfarin alone). Evaluate if Sertraline benefits outweigh risks. Refer to falls clinic. Caregiver education on safety measures.'),
-- CONTRADICTION NOTE 1: M010 — note says patient continues Digoxin, but structured data says discontinued
('N025','M010','E025','2025-01-25','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 87 y/o male with complex cardiac history presents for heart failure management. Currently taking 10+ medications. Patient reports fatigue and mild ankle swelling. Continues on Digoxin 0.125mg daily — states he has been taking it without interruption. Denies missed doses of any medications. OBJECTIVE: BP 110/68, HR 62 regular. JVD +2cm. Bilateral ankle edema 1+. BNP 1250 (elevated). ASSESSMENT: 1. Heart failure — NYHA class III, suboptimally controlled. BNP significantly elevated. 2. CKD stage 4 — eGFR 22, creatinine 2.8. Requires dose adjustment consideration. 3. Polypharmacy — 10 active medications, HIGH risk. 4. Note: Patient reports continuing Digoxin which warrants verification with pharmacy records. Digoxin level should be monitored closely given renal function. PLAN: Increase Furosemide. Check Digoxin level. Renal function monitoring. Follow-up 2 weeks.'),
-- CONTRADICTION NOTE 2: M014 — note says NKDA (no known drug allergies) but diagnosis includes documented allergy reaction
('N036','M014','E036','2025-01-05','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 89 y/o male with advanced Alzheimer disease. Caregiver reports worsening behavioral disturbance — hitting, yelling at night. NKDA. Currently on 8 medications including Lorazepam 0.5mg BID for agitation. OBJECTIVE: Agitated during exam. MMSE not testable. Gait requires 2-person assist. ASSESSMENT: 1. Alzheimer disease with behavioral disturbance — worsening despite Lorazepam. 2. CRITICAL CONCERN: Lorazepam in elderly dementia patient — Beers Criteria inappropriate, increases fall risk and paradoxical agitation. 3. Polypharmacy — 8 active medications. Aspirin + Lorazepam combination is concerning in fall-risk patient. PLAN: Taper Lorazepam gradually over 4 weeks. Consider low-dose quetiapine for behavioral management. Urgent fall risk assessment. Goals of care discussion with family.'),
('N004','M002','E004','2025-01-20','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 87 y/o male presents for coronary artery disease follow-up. Reports stable exertional symptoms. Walking 1 block before mild dyspnea. No chest pain. Compliance with 8 medications reported. OBJECTIVE: BP 128/76, HR 64 regular. No JVD. Lungs clear. ASSESSMENT: 1. CAD — stable on current medical therapy. 2. HFrEF — NYHA class II-III. 3. Type 2 DM — HbA1c 8.1%, above target. 4. A-fib — rate controlled on Metoprolol + Digoxin. INR therapeutic. PLAN: Increase Glipizide to 10mg. Continue current cardiac regimen. A1C recheck in 3 months.'),
('N010','M004','E010','2025-01-15','progress','Dr. Michael Ross','SUBJECTIVE: 84 y/o male with COPD presents for follow-up after recent exacerbation. Reports improved breathing since completing prednisone burst. Using rescue inhaler 2-3x/day (down from 6x/day). Continues Tiotropium daily. OBJECTIVE: Lungs with mild expiratory wheezing bilaterally. O2 sat 92% on room air. FEV1 1.2L (38% predicted). ASSESSMENT: 1. COPD — severe, improving from exacerbation but baseline is poor. 2. CAD — stable on Metoprolol, Lisinopril, Atorvastatin. 3. Polypharmacy — 7 medications. PLAN: Continue maintenance prednisone 10mg. Pulmonary rehab referral. Continue cardiac medications.'),
('N043','M016','E043','2025-05-05','discharge','Dr. Michael Ross','DISCHARGE SUMMARY: 83 y/o male admitted with acute STEMI involving LAD. Emergent PCI with DES placement to proximal LAD — successful with TIMI 3 flow. Peak Troponin I 15.2 ng/mL. Echo shows EF 40% with anterior wall hypokinesis. Started on dual antiplatelet therapy (Aspirin 81mg + Clopidogrel 75mg). Atorvastatin increased to 80mg. DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS: Metoprolol 50mg BID, Clopidogrel 75mg daily, Atorvastatin 80mg daily, Metformin 1000mg BID, Lisinopril 20mg daily, Aspirin 81mg daily. Cardiac rehab referral. Follow-up in 1 week.'),
('N049','M020','E049','2025-01-12','progress','Dr. Michael Ross','SUBJECTIVE: 89 y/o male with HFrEF (EF 25%), CAD, DM, CKD stage 4. Reports increasing fatigue and SOB with minimal exertion. Weight up 5 lbs in 2 weeks despite fluid restriction. Currently on 9 medications. OBJECTIVE: BP 98/60, HR 78 irregular. JVD +4cm. Bilateral LE edema 3+. Lungs with bibasilar crackles. ASSESSMENT: 1. Decompensated heart failure — volume overloaded despite high-dose diuretics. 2. CKD stage 4 — eGFR 18, limiting diuretic efficacy (cardiorenal syndrome). 3. Polypharmacy — 9 medications, multiple drug interactions concern (Warfarin + Aspirin bleeding risk, K+ from Spironolactone + ACE-I + CKD). 4. Hyperkalemia risk — K+ 5.8 on last check. PLAN: Admit for IV diuresis. Hold Spironolactone given hyperkalemia risk. Nephrology consult. Discuss goals of care — advanced illness trajectory.'),
('N065','M027','E065','2025-05-01','progress','Dr. Emily Park','SUBJECTIVE: 71 y/o female presents for annual wellness visit. No complaints. No chronic conditions. No medications. Active, walks 3 miles daily. OBJECTIVE: BP 118/72, HR 68. BMI 23. All systems normal. ASSESSMENT: Healthy female, no chronic diseases. PLAN: Age-appropriate screenings. Mammogram, colonoscopy due. Flu and pneumonia vaccines. Return in 1 year.'),
('N083','M038','E083','2025-06-01','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 70 y/o male presents for annual physical. No medications. No chronic conditions. Reports occasional lower back pain with prolonged sitting. OBJECTIVE: BP 122/78, HR 72. Normal exam. ASSESSMENT: Healthy male. Mechanical low back pain. PLAN: Core strengthening exercises. Age-appropriate cancer screenings. Return PRN or 1 year.');

-- ============================================================
-- POLICY DOCUMENTS (~30 documents)
-- ============================================================
INSERT INTO POLICY_DOCUMENT VALUES
('PD001','Polypharmacy Review Protocol','coverage','CMS Guidelines','polypharmacy','2024-01-01','POLYPHARMACY REVIEW PROTOCOL: Members aged 65+ taking 5 or more concurrent active medications require an annual Comprehensive Medication Review (CMR). Triggers for expedited review: (1) Any member taking 8+ medications, (2) Member with 2+ falls in 6 months on 5+ medications, (3) New hospital admission with medication-related adverse event. Required actions: Pharmacist-led medication therapy management (MTM) session, documentation of each medication''s indication, identification of potentially inappropriate medications per Beers Criteria, deprescribing recommendations with timeline, and follow-up within 30 days. Members with polypharmacy-related adverse events must be flagged for care coordination intervention.'),
('PD002','Warfarin Management Policy','formulary','Plan Formulary Committee','anticoagulation','2024-01-01','WARFARIN MANAGEMENT: Warfarin is formulary-approved for atrial fibrillation, DVT/PE, and mechanical heart valves. Requirements: (1) INR monitoring every 4-6 weeks when stable, weekly when initiating or adjusting dose. (2) Target INR 2.0-3.0 for most indications, 2.5-3.5 for mechanical valves. (3) Concurrent use of Warfarin + Aspirin requires documented clinical justification (e.g., recent ACS with A-fib) and must be reviewed every 6 months. (4) Members on Warfarin with CKD stage 4+ require monthly INR monitoring. (5) Supratherapeutic INR >4.0 triggers mandatory provider notification within 24 hours. DOAC alternatives (Apixaban, Rivarelbaan) should be considered for members with labile INR (time in therapeutic range <60%).'),
('PD003','Benzodiazepine Use in Elderly','prior-auth','Pharmacy & Therapeutics','benzodiazepines','2024-01-01','PRIOR AUTHORIZATION REQUIRED: Benzodiazepines (including Lorazepam, Alprazolam, Diazepam, Clonazepam) in members aged 65+. Per Beers Criteria 2023, benzodiazepines are potentially inappropriate in older adults due to: increased risk of cognitive impairment, delirium, falls, fractures, and motor vehicle accidents. Approval criteria: (1) Trial and failure of at least 2 non-benzodiazepine alternatives, (2) Documented indication (seizure disorder, severe alcohol withdrawal, or anesthesia-related), (3) Maximum 30-day supply with mandatory reassessment, (4) NOT approved for: insomnia, generalized anxiety (first-line), or behavioral symptoms of dementia. Members currently on chronic benzodiazepines require taper plan within 90 days of review.'),
('PD004','Statin Therapy Guidelines','step-therapy','Pharmacy & Therapeutics','statins','2024-01-01','STEP THERAPY: Statin medications. Step 1 (preferred): Generic Atorvastatin or Rosuvastatin. Step 2 (if intolerant to Step 1): Generic Pravastatin or Simvastatin. Step 3 (if intolerant to Steps 1-2): Brand-name alternatives require prior auth with documentation of statin intolerance (myalgia, elevated CK >5x ULN, or documented rhabdomyolysis). For ASCVD patients: High-intensity statin required (Atorvastatin 40-80mg or Rosuvastatin 20-40mg). For diabetes patients without ASCVD: Moderate-intensity statin (Atorvastatin 10-20mg or Rosuvastatin 5-10mg). Post-ACS: Atorvastatin 80mg is the preferred agent per AHA/ACC guidelines.'),
('PD005','CKD Medication Adjustment Protocol','coverage','Nephrology Advisory Board','renal','2024-06-01','CKD MEDICATION ADJUSTMENT: Members with eGFR <45 mL/min require medication dose review. Key adjustments: (1) Metformin: Hold if eGFR <30; reduce dose by 50% if eGFR 30-45; monitor lactic acidosis symptoms. (2) Digoxin: Reduce dose; target serum level 0.5-0.9 ng/mL in CKD (lower than standard). Levels >2.0 ng/mL in CKD patients is TOXIC and requires immediate intervention. (3) NSAIDs: CONTRAINDICATED in eGFR <30. (4) ACE inhibitors/ARBs: Continue but monitor potassium closely; hold if K+ >5.5. (5) Warfarin: Increased bleeding risk in CKD; consider reduced target INR range (2.0-2.5) in CKD stage 4+. (6) All renally cleared medications require pharmacy review before initiation.'),
('PD006','Heart Failure Medication Protocol','coverage','Cardiology Advisory Board','heart_failure','2024-01-01','HEART FAILURE MANAGEMENT: Evidence-based medication protocol for HFrEF (EF ≤40%). Required medications (unless contraindicated): (1) ACE inhibitor or ARB (Lisinopril preferred), (2) Beta-blocker (Metoprolol Succinate, Carvedilol, or Bisoprolol), (3) Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (Spironolactone or Eplerenone) if EF ≤35% and K+ <5.0, (4) SGLT2 inhibitor (Dapagliflozin or Empagliflozin). Diuretics (Furosemide) for volume management. MONITORING: Renal function and electrolytes within 1-2 weeks of initiation/dose change. BNP trending to guide therapy. Digoxin considered only if symptoms persist despite optimal GDMT — target level 0.5-0.9 ng/mL.'),
('PD007','Opioid Prescribing Policy','prior-auth','Pain Management Committee','opioids','2024-01-01','OPIOID PRESCRIBING: Prior authorization required for opioid prescriptions >7 days. Requirements: documented pain assessment, trial of non-opioid alternatives (NSAIDs, acetaminophen, physical therapy), signed opioid agreement, prescription drug monitoring program (PDMP) check within 24 hours, and naloxone co-prescribed for doses ≥50 MME/day. Concurrent benzodiazepine + opioid requires additional PA with documented medical necessity.'),
('PD008','Diabetes Management Protocol','coverage','Endocrinology Advisory Board','diabetes','2024-01-01','TYPE 2 DIABETES MANAGEMENT: First-line: Metformin (if eGFR ≥30). Second-line additions based on comorbidities: (1) With ASCVD: GLP-1 RA (Semaglutide, Liraglutide) or SGLT2i, (2) With HF: SGLT2i preferred, (3) With CKD: SGLT2i or GLP-1 RA. Insulin: Add basal insulin if HbA1c remains >9% despite dual oral therapy. Target HbA1c: <7% for most adults, <8% for elderly (>75) with comorbidities or limited life expectancy. Monitoring: HbA1c every 3 months until stable, then every 6 months.'),
('PD009','Fall Prevention Program','coverage','Geriatrics Advisory Board','falls','2024-01-01','FALL PREVENTION: Members aged 65+ with documented fall or fall risk require: (1) Annual fall risk assessment using Timed Up and Go (TUG) test, (2) Medication review — flag fall-risk medications (benzodiazepines, anticholinergics, sedating antihistamines, alpha-blockers, high-dose diuretics), (3) Orthostatic hypotension screening if on antihypertensives, (4) Home safety evaluation referral if 2+ falls in 12 months, (5) Physical therapy referral for balance and strength training. Members on 3+ fall-risk medications require care coordination intervention.'),
('PD010','Anticholinergic Burden Assessment','coverage','Geriatrics Advisory Board','anticholinergics','2024-01-01','ANTICHOLINERGIC BURDEN: All members aged 65+ should have anticholinergic burden assessed annually. High anticholinergic burden (ACB score ≥3) associated with cognitive decline, delirium, and falls. Common high-burden medications: Diphenhydramine, Oxybutynin, Amitriptyline, Paroxetine. Required: Calculate ACB score at each CMR, flag for pharmacist intervention if ACB ≥3, document deprescribing plan or clinical justification for continuation.'),
('PD011','Formulary Exception Process','prior-auth','Pharmacy & Therapeutics','formulary','2024-01-01','FORMULARY EXCEPTION: Non-formulary medication requests require: (1) Clinical rationale documenting medical necessity, (2) Documentation of formulary alternative trials and failures, (3) Peer-reviewed literature supporting off-formulary use when applicable. Turnaround: Standard 72 hours, Urgent 24 hours. Appeals process available within 30 days of denial.'),
('PD012','Dual Antiplatelet Therapy Policy','coverage','Cardiology Advisory Board','antiplatelet','2024-06-01','DUAL ANTIPLATELET THERAPY (DAPT): Post-PCI with DES: Aspirin 81mg + Clopidogrel 75mg (or Ticagrelor 90mg BID) for minimum 12 months. Extended DAPT beyond 12 months requires documented risk-benefit assessment using DAPT score. In patients with high bleeding risk (age >75, CKD, concurrent anticoagulation): shortened DAPT of 3-6 months may be considered. Triple therapy (DAPT + anticoagulant) limited to 1 month then step down to dual pathway (anticoagulant + single antiplatelet).'),
('PD013','Proton Pump Inhibitor Review','coverage','Pharmacy & Therapeutics','PPI','2024-01-01','PPI LONG-TERM USE REVIEW: Members on PPIs (Omeprazole, Pantoprazole, etc.) for >8 weeks require: (1) Documented indication for continued use, (2) Step-down trial assessment (reduce dose or switch to H2 blocker), (3) Monitoring for long-term risks: hypomagnesemia, C. difficile, bone density loss, vitamin B12 deficiency. Annual review required for chronic PPI users.'),
('PD014','Medication Therapy Management','coverage','CMS Guidelines','MTM','2024-01-01','MEDICATION THERAPY MANAGEMENT (MTM): Eligible members meeting CMS criteria (3+ chronic conditions, 8+ Part D medications, >$4,935 annual drug cost) receive comprehensive medication review annually. MTM includes: medication reconciliation, drug interaction screening, adherence assessment, cost optimization (generic substitution where appropriate), and personalized medication action plan.');

-- ============================================================
-- DRUG INTERACTION GUIDELINES (~30 guidelines)
-- ============================================================
INSERT INTO DRUG_INTERACTION_GUIDELINE VALUES
('DIG001','Warfarin + Aspirin','major','FDA/Micromedex','Increased bleeding risk','INTERACTION: Warfarin + Aspirin. SEVERITY: Major. MECHANISM: Additive anticoagulant/antiplatelet effects. Aspirin inhibits platelet aggregation; Warfarin inhibits clotting factor synthesis. Combined use significantly increases risk of GI bleeding (2-3x), intracranial hemorrhage, and other hemorrhagic events. MANAGEMENT: Combination should only be used when clinical benefit clearly outweighs risk (e.g., recent ACS with concurrent A-fib). If combined: use lowest effective Aspirin dose (81mg), maintain INR at lower end of therapeutic range (2.0-2.5), add GI protection (PPI), and monitor for signs of bleeding (melena, hematuria, bruising). Reassess need for dual therapy every 6 months.'),
('DIG002','Warfarin + Omeprazole','moderate','FDA/Micromedex','Altered Warfarin metabolism','INTERACTION: Warfarin + Omeprazole. SEVERITY: Moderate. MECHANISM: Omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19, which can alter Warfarin metabolism and increase INR. MANAGEMENT: Monitor INR more frequently (every 2 weeks for first month) when adding or discontinuing Omeprazole. Consider Pantoprazole as alternative PPI (less CYP interaction). Adjust Warfarin dose based on INR trends. Generally safe to use together with appropriate monitoring.'),
('DIG003','Metformin + CKD (eGFR <30)','contraindicated','FDA Black Box','Lactic acidosis risk','INTERACTION: Metformin in severe renal impairment (eGFR <30). SEVERITY: Contraindicated. MECHANISM: Metformin is renally cleared. Impaired clearance leads to accumulation and increased risk of lactic acidosis — a rare but potentially fatal complication. MANAGEMENT: DISCONTINUE Metformin if eGFR <30. Reduce dose by 50% if eGFR 30-45. Monitor renal function at least quarterly. Educate patient on symptoms of lactic acidosis: muscle pain, weakness, difficulty breathing, unusual fatigue. Alternative: Consider DPP-4 inhibitors (Sitagliptin, dose-adjusted) or insulin therapy.'),
('DIG004','Digoxin + Furosemide','major','FDA/Micromedex','Hypokalemia-enhanced Digoxin toxicity','INTERACTION: Digoxin + Furosemide. SEVERITY: Major. MECHANISM: Furosemide causes potassium loss. Hypokalemia enhances Digoxin binding to cardiac Na+/K+ ATPase, dramatically increasing risk of Digoxin toxicity (arrhythmias, nausea, visual disturbances). MANAGEMENT: Monitor potassium level closely (maintain K+ ≥4.0 mEq/L). Check Digoxin level regularly (target 0.5-0.9 ng/mL for heart failure). Consider potassium supplementation or potassium-sparing diuretic (Spironolactone). Signs of toxicity: nausea, vomiting, yellow-green visual changes, bradycardia, arrhythmias.'),
('DIG005','ACE Inhibitor + Spironolactone','major','FDA/Micromedex','Hyperkalemia risk','INTERACTION: ACE Inhibitor (Lisinopril) + Spironolactone. SEVERITY: Major. MECHANISM: Both medications independently raise serum potassium. Combined use significantly increases hyperkalemia risk, especially in CKD and elderly patients. MANAGEMENT: Monitor potassium within 1 week of initiation and regularly thereafter. Hold if K+ >5.5 mEq/L. Contraindicated in combination with eGFR <30. Risk factors: CKD, diabetes, advanced age, high-dose ACE-I. Educate on symptoms: muscle weakness, palpitations, paresthesias.'),
('DIG006','Warfarin + Metformin','minor','FDA/Micromedex','Minimal interaction','INTERACTION: Warfarin + Metformin. SEVERITY: Minor. MECHANISM: Metformin may minimally affect Warfarin protein binding. MANAGEMENT: No dose adjustment typically needed. Standard INR monitoring sufficient. Clinically insignificant in most patients.'),
('DIG007','Lorazepam + Donepezil','major','Clinical Guidelines','Opposing pharmacological effects','INTERACTION: Lorazepam + Donepezil. SEVERITY: Major. MECHANISM: Pharmacological antagonism. Donepezil enhances cholinergic activity to improve cognition in dementia. Lorazepam (and all benzodiazepines) impair cognition, memory, and psychomotor function, directly counteracting Donepezil''s benefits. Additionally, both increase fall risk in elderly. MANAGEMENT: AVOID combination when possible. Benzodiazepines are listed as Potentially Inappropriate Medications (PIMs) in dementia patients per Beers Criteria. If absolutely required: use lowest dose, shortest duration, with informed consent from caregiver. Prefer non-benzodiazepine alternatives for agitation/anxiety in dementia (low-dose quetiapine, trazodone, non-pharmacologic interventions).'),
('DIG008','Digoxin + Amiodarone','major','FDA/Micromedex','Increased Digoxin levels','INTERACTION: Digoxin + Amiodarone. SEVERITY: Major. MECHANISM: Amiodarone inhibits P-glycoprotein and renal clearance of Digoxin, increasing serum Digoxin levels by 50-100%. MANAGEMENT: Reduce Digoxin dose by 50% when starting Amiodarone. Monitor Digoxin levels within 1 week and regularly. Target Digoxin level 0.5-0.9 ng/mL. Watch for toxicity signs.'),
('DIG009','Insulin + Metformin','moderate','Clinical Guidelines','Additive hypoglycemia risk','INTERACTION: Insulin + Metformin. SEVERITY: Moderate. MECHANISM: Both lower blood glucose through different mechanisms. Combined use increases hypoglycemia risk, particularly in CKD patients where Metformin clearance is reduced. MANAGEMENT: Monitor blood glucose frequently. Adjust insulin dose based on glucose trends. Metformin dose adjustment required in CKD (eGFR 30-45: max 1000mg/day; eGFR <30: discontinue).'),
('DIG010','Warfarin + Atorvastatin','moderate','FDA/Micromedex','Increased INR','INTERACTION: Warfarin + Atorvastatin. SEVERITY: Moderate. MECHANISM: Atorvastatin may potentiate Warfarin effect via CYP3A4 competition. Statin-related liver effects can alter clotting factor production. MANAGEMENT: Monitor INR when initiating or changing Atorvastatin dose. Adjust Warfarin if needed. Usually manageable with routine monitoring.'),
('DIG011','Clopidogrel + Omeprazole','major','FDA/Micromedex','Reduced Clopidogrel efficacy','INTERACTION: Clopidogrel + Omeprazole. SEVERITY: Major. MECHANISM: Omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19, the enzyme required to convert Clopidogrel to its active metabolite. This can reduce Clopidogrel''s antiplatelet effect by 25-50%, increasing risk of stent thrombosis or recurrent MI. MANAGEMENT: AVOID Omeprazole with Clopidogrel. Use Pantoprazole instead (minimal CYP2C19 inhibition). If PPI required, Pantoprazole 40mg daily is the recommended alternative. FDA black-box warning against this combination.'),
('DIG012','Furosemide + Lisinopril','moderate','Clinical Guidelines','First-dose hypotension and renal effects','INTERACTION: Furosemide + Lisinopril. SEVERITY: Moderate. MECHANISM: Furosemide-induced volume depletion can potentiate first-dose hypotension with ACE inhibitors and worsen renal function. MANAGEMENT: Monitor blood pressure, renal function, and electrolytes. Start ACE-I at low dose if on high-dose diuretic. Watch for orthostatic hypotension symptoms.'),
('DIG013','Metoprolol + Digoxin','moderate','FDA/Micromedex','Additive bradycardia','INTERACTION: Metoprolol + Digoxin. SEVERITY: Moderate. MECHANISM: Both agents slow heart rate through different mechanisms (beta-blockade and vagal enhancement). Combined use increases risk of symptomatic bradycardia and heart block. MANAGEMENT: Monitor heart rate regularly. Target HR 60-80 bpm. ECG if HR <50 or symptoms of bradycardia (dizziness, syncope, fatigue). Dose reduction of one or both agents may be needed.'),
('DIG014','Aspirin + Clopidogrel','moderate','AHA/ACC Guidelines','Dual antiplatelet — bleeding risk','INTERACTION: Aspirin + Clopidogrel (DAPT). SEVERITY: Moderate. MECHANISM: Both inhibit platelet aggregation through different pathways, providing synergistic antiplatelet effect but also additive bleeding risk. MANAGEMENT: Standard post-PCI therapy. Duration per guidelines (minimum 12 months post-DES). Use lowest effective Aspirin dose (81mg). Add PPI for GI protection (Pantoprazole preferred). Monitor for bleeding. Shorter DAPT in high bleeding risk.'),
('DIG015','Spironolactone + Potassium supplements','major','FDA/Micromedex','Life-threatening hyperkalemia','INTERACTION: Spironolactone + Potassium supplements. SEVERITY: Major — potentially life-threatening. MECHANISM: Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic. Adding potassium supplements can cause dangerous hyperkalemia (cardiac arrhythmias, cardiac arrest). MANAGEMENT: Generally AVOID combination. If both necessary: frequent K+ monitoring (weekly initially), keep K+ <5.0 mEq/L, immediate intervention if K+ >5.5.'),
('DIG016','Sertraline + Warfarin','moderate','FDA/Micromedex','Increased bleeding via serotonin and CYP effects','INTERACTION: Sertraline + Warfarin. SEVERITY: Moderate. MECHANISM: SSRIs impair platelet aggregation via serotonin depletion in platelets. Sertraline also inhibits CYP2C9, potentially increasing Warfarin levels. Combined bleeding risk is additive. MANAGEMENT: Monitor INR closely when starting or stopping Sertraline. Consider lower Warfarin dose. Watch for bruising, GI bleeding, or other hemorrhagic signs.');
